<Hub pillarId="safety-debates">
  <p>
    Two uses of almost the same words. Do not mix them.
    <a href="/glossary/#super-intelligence">Super Intelligence (SI), still widely called AI</a>, is the executive order’s name for the systems US law already calls artificial intelligence.
    Research “superintelligence,” often one word, is a hypothetical system beyond human performance.
    The careful report uses tighter words than either slogan, and the experts in it do not agree. We will not sand that into a pitch.
  </p>
  <h2>What does the executive order mean by Super Intelligence?</h2>
  <p>
    It means the technologies and systems already covered by “artificial intelligence” in 15 U.S.C. § 9401(3), and it tells federal executive agencies to say SI instead of AI.
    <a href="https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/02/2026-20321/inaugurating-the-era-of-super-intelligence">Executive Order 14434</a>, “Inaugurating the Era of Super Intelligence,” was signed on 29 September 2026 and published in the Federal Register on 2 October 2026.
    Section 2 directs executive departments and agencies, to the maximum extent permitted by law, to use “Super Intelligence” and “SI” in official non-statutory documents.
    Section 3 says that, for the order, those terms mean the statutory category. That is today’s legal category, not a new kind of machine.
    <a href="https://www.techrepublic.com/article/news-trump-super-intelligence-ai-executive-order/">TechRepublic</a> reports that the order does not directly require private companies to rename products or stop saying AI.
  </p>
  <h2>What do researchers mean by superintelligence?</h2>
  <p>
    They mean a hypothetical system that would do a wide range of tasks at a level no human can match, and a DeepMind paper says that level has not been achieved.
    Morris and colleagues, in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.02462">Levels of AGI</a>, define artificial superintelligence as Level 5 general AI: systems able to do a wide range of tasks at a level that no human can match.
    They define “superhuman” performance as outperforming 100 percent of humans, and their table marks artificial superintelligence as not yet achieved.
    That is a research ceiling. It is not the executive order’s SI.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/sites/default/files/2026-02/international-ai-safety-report-2026.pdf">International AI Safety Report 2026</a> does not use the order’s label for that ceiling.
    It defines artificial general intelligence as a hypothetical model or system that equals or surpasses human performance on all or almost all cognitive tasks.
    It defines a loss-of-control scenario as one in which one or more general-purpose systems operate outside of anyone’s control, with no clear path to regaining control.
    Public talk often collapses those ideas into one loud word. The report does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report says some experts give credence to outcomes as severe as the marginalisation or extinction of humanity, and that other experts consider such scenarios implausible. Disagreement, it says, comes from disagreements about future capabilities, behavioural propensities, and how systems will be deployed. We are not going to pretend that split is closed.
  </p>
  <h2>What does the report say about today?</h2>
  <p>
    Current systems, the report says, show early signs of relevant capabilities, but not at levels that would enable loss of control. A system would need to evade oversight, carry out long-term plans, and stop people from putting countermeasures in place. The report says loss of control becomes more likely if a system is misaligned: if it uses its capabilities in ways that conflict with the intentions of developers, users, or society. Alignment, in the report’s glossary, is the propensity to use capabilities in line with human intentions, values, or norms. The report calls alignment an open scientific problem.
    Those current systems fall under the executive order’s Super Intelligence, because the order uses the statutory definition of artificial intelligence. They are not the research ceiling above.
  </p>
  <p>
    Present harm is a different chapter. The report says current systems already fail in ordinary ways, including fabricated information, and that malicious actors already use generated content for scams. Those are documented. A future loss of control is a risk the report treats as uncertain in likelihood and potentially extreme in severity.
  </p>
  <p>
    NIST’s <a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework">AI Risk Management Framework</a>, released on 26 January 2023, is a voluntary way for organisations to manage risks and to build trustworthiness into design and use. It is not a verdict on the long-term debate. The <a href="https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles">OECD principles</a> ask for AI that is robust, secure, and safe, and for accountability. They are standards for practice, not a prediction. The OECD’s word remains “AI.”
  </p>
  <p>Words: <a href="/glossary/#super-intelligence">Super Intelligence (SI)</a>, <a href="/glossary/#artificial-general-intelligence">AGI</a>, <a href="/glossary/#alignment">alignment</a>, <a href="/glossary/#loss-of-control">loss of control</a>.</p>
